Let me check the current state of the code in the repo and understand what the actual vulnerability is. The description says "memory allocation occurs even when the function fails." Let me look at the `redisvFormatCommand` function more carefully to understand the memory allocation issue.

Looking at the code again:

```c
cleanup:
    if (curargv) {
        while(argc--)
            sdsfree(curargv[argc]);
        hi_free(curargv);
    }

    sdsfree(curarg);
    hi_free(cmd);

    return error_type;
```

The issue is that `cmd` is allocated with `hi_malloc(totlen+1)` and if the function fails after that, `cmd` is freed. But the description says "memory allocation occurs even when the function fails."

Wait, let me re-read the description: "A vulnerability exists in redisFormatCommand() where memory allocation occurs even when the function fails."

This could mean that when `redisFormatCommand` fails, memory is still allocated (leaked), OR that the function allocates memory even when it's going to fail.

Actually, looking at the fuzzer fix commit `1abe0c8`, the issue is that when `redisFormatCommand` fails, `cmd` is not set to NULL, so the fuzzer tries to free an uninitialized pointer. This is the actual vulnerability being tested.

